body:
Hello! Please direct me to a more appropriate place if applicable, I couldn't find any documentation to the contrary.

It seems that while several string-only APIs have been getting more appropriate object models (like `className` -> `classList`, `style` -> `computedStyleMap`) or more appropriate methods (`URL` and `URLSearchParams`), one API which has remained a sore spot for a long time is `document.cookie`.

We have a long list of 3rd-party libraries more or less implementing any one of the aforementioned APIs, and it may be time to consider a native interface for cookie handling.

In Chrome & Firefox (among others), extensions have been getting proper cookie support for several years now, so they may be a convenient place to look at as far as browser compatibility and API design is concerned.

Borrowing from both Web Extension APIs and mixing in a bit of the other set-based DOM APIs, one might conjure something which looks like this:

```javascript
/*
Current request cookies:

logged_in=1;
	Domain=example.com;
	Path=/;
	Expires=Tue, Jun 20 2028 22:00:00 GMT
id=a3fWa;
	Domain=example.com;
	Path=/;
	Secure;
	Expires=Wed, 21 Oct 2015 07:28:00 GMT;
secretAuth=hunter2;
	Domain=example.com;
	Path=/;
	Secure;
	HttpOnly;
	Expires=Tue, Jun 20 2028 23:00:00 GMT
*/

var jar = document.cookieJar;

jar.get('logged_in');
// Cookie object:
({
	name: 'logged_in',
	value: '1',
	domain: 'example.com',
	path: '/',
	httpOnly: true,
	secure: false,
	sameSite: false,
	expirationDate: Date(1845151200000)
})

[...jar.keys()]; // ['logged_in', 'id']
[...jar.values()]; // [{ name: 'logged_in ', ... }, { name: 'id', ... }]
[...jar.entries()]; // [ ['logged_in', { ... }], ['id', { ... }] ]

jar.set({
	name: 'something imaginative',
	value: 'wow this is a spectacular example',
	domain: 'subdomain.example.com',
	path: '/subpath',
	secure: false
});

[...jar.keys()]; // ['logged_in', 'id', 'something imaginative']

jar.has('id'); // true

// Constructor functions:
new Cookie(`logged_in=1;
	Domain=example.com;
	Path=/;
	HttpOnly;
	Expires=Tue, Jun 20 2028 22:00:00 GMT`);
// => same cookie object as the first example

new CookieJar(['cookieString', 'anotherCookieString']);
```

That's a small example from the JavaScript side of things. We have an object which looks a lot like a Map and appropriate constructors.

What do you think? Will this be a useful addition? Has something similar been discussed before?
